 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study confirmed 2 of the 5 cases it was asked for. Both are on different scenes, and both slow the recorded lead car to 0.5x with no time shift: S24 on 12855a41 and S6 on 096988dd. Every repeat at these two settings failed. However, the triage suggests that neither is a clear case of the policy rear-ending the car ahead. At S6, each collision was flagged at the moment of handoff, while the ego was still replaying the recorded drive, so the policy was not at fault. At S24, contact came about 0.5 s after handoff with the ego still on the recorded path, and fault is unclear. The setup, not the 2 Hz policy, is the more likely cause of these failures. No other setting failed more than once. The settings that were repeated without any failure hold the failure rate below about one half, but most settings got only one run, which is a hint rather than a rate.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "On scene 12855a41, slowing the lead car to 0.5x with no time shift failed in every repeat. This setting meets the goal's bar, but each contact came about 0.5 s after the policy took over, with the ego still on the recorded human path. Whether the policy was at fault is unclear: the replay set up most of the closing, and one run may be a box-size overlap.",
    "settings": [
     "S24"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "On scene 096988dd, slowing the lead truck to 0.5x with no time shift failed in every repeat and meets the goal's bar. The triage found every one of these collisions was flagged at the moment of handoff, while the ego was still replaying the log. So these are failures of the replay setup, not of the policy.",
    "settings": [
     "S6"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The only failure the policy clearly caused was not a rear-end. On 12855a41 with the lead at 0.75x, the ego drifted left into the next lane and sideswiped a car there, which fits the known left drift. This is a single run, so it is a hint, not a rate.",
    "settings": [
     "S25"
    ]
   },
   {
